592186045436] [17592186049593 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428906 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186049593 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428907 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186049593 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428908 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186049593 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428909 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186049593 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428910 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186049593 #edb/ref 1031 "Defining Continuity at a Point" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049593 #edb/ref 1033 314 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049593 #edb/ref 1034 0.4973280634379766 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186049593 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "030b6230-d213-52b6-874d-139dc628effc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127711 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186504070 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127711 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186504187 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127711 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186504318 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127711 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186504332 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127711 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186504469 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127711 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127711 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127711 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186504070 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127711 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ba6bec9c-e43c-5c8c-a9dd-a2c9d29fe6c0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128903 #edb/ref 1069 "e-3585" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128903 #edb/ref 1071 "For the function $y=f(x)$ below, where does the function have a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote?\n\n![](images/1a/1aeebec4df41080efc0d91a8adf00a799828e793e731ed7a36d32d881110596b.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128903 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "001529aa-c97a-5d69-82fb-cd4319a4dd7d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128903 #edb/ref 1074 "The function has discontinuities due to vertical asymptotes at $x=\\pm 1.$ Note that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{+}} f(x) = -\\infty,\n$$\n\nwhich is enough to conclude that there is a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=-1.$ Similarly\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 1^{-}} f(x) = \\infty,\n$$\n\nwhich is enough to conclude that there is a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=1.$\n\nThere is also a discontinuity at $x=-2,$ but this is a point discontinuity, not a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128904 #edb/ref 1069 "e-3586" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128904 #edb/ref 1071 "Find all the points where the function $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x^2-5x+6}$ has infinite discontinuities." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128904 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "58f2bf5b-04b1-5931-8452-c095b55722ed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128904 #edb/ref 1074 "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ \n\nTo find the vertical asymptotes, let's start by factoring the expression in the denominator. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-5x+6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(x-3)(x-2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we can find the vertical asymptotes by setting the denominator to zero:\n\n$$\n(x-3)(x-2) = 0 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow\\qquad x=2,3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has infinite discontinuities at $x=2$ and $x=3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128905 #edb/ref 1069 "e-3587" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128905 #edb/ref 1071 "Find all the points where the function $f(x) = \\dfrac{x^2+x-6}{x^2-4}$ has infinite discontinuities." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128905 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "26d318a3-0b3f-50be-9802-92b10b83d119" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128905 #edb/ref 1074 "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ \n\nTo find the vertical asymptotes, let's start by factoring the expressions in the numerator and denominator. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+x-6}{{x}^{2}-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-2)(x+3)}{(x-2)(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(x-2)}(x+3)}{\\cancel{(x-2)}(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+3}{x+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the vertical asymptotes, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\nx+2 = 0 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow\\qquad x=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has an infinite discontinuity at $x=-2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128906 #edb/ref 1069 "e-3588" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128906 #edb/ref 1071 "Find all of the points where $f(x)$ below has infinite discontinuities.\n\n![](images/2b/2b1f430a78861fce15c689843d0e4af5376e0f600b650b0a614257d86365cd29.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128906 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "83f4e413-e2f4-53dd-9893-fdf8e2df2642" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128906 #edb/ref 1074 "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$ The only possible way that $y=f(x)$ can have vertical asymptotes is if either $\\dfrac{1}{x-5}$ or $\\dfrac{1}{x-4}$ grow without bound within their respective subdomains.\n\n- We first compute the asymptotes of $\\dfrac{1}{x-5}.$ Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\nx-5=0\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x=5.\n$$\n\nAlthough this branch of the function is defined for $x < 5$, we do have that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 5^{-}}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{x-5}\\right) = -\\infty,\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude that $x=5$ is indeed an infinite discontinuity.\n\n- Next, we compute the asymptotes of $\\dfrac{1}{x-4}.$ Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n\n$$\nx-4=0\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x=4.\n$$\n\nHowever, this branch of the function is defined for $x \\geq 5,$ which does not contain the neighborhood of $x=4.$ So $x=4$ cannot be a vertical asymptote, and therefore $x=4$ cannot be an infinite discontinuity.\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ only has a single infinite discontinuity, and it is at $x=5.$\n\nThis matches up with what we see in the graph of $y=f(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/c1/c1e68cf3a