17592186129906 #edb/ref 1061 12113 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129906 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8acbfdc6-b18f-5290-b574-bb7515ced757" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129906 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129089 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129907 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186129908 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129907 #edb/ref 1061 12114 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129907 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bf945475-7921-5714-a012-fd5bfdd13e6b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129907 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129090 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129908 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186129909 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129908 #edb/ref 1061 12115 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129908 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "18da17af-7589-58b8-ac89-6eded53c96a2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129908 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129091 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129909 #edb/ref 1061 12116 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129909 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "23a64c25-6876-5b6c-9e24-a82e1cee59ce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129909 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129092 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130330 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's take a look at the function $y=\\dfrac 1 x$ below.\n\n![](images/cc/cc9593860b0c134d0d99635b37eeac4f16a712a7af9e1c6474fcf1f885e8451e.png)\n\nThe function has a vertical asymptote at $x=0.$ There is also a discontinuity in the function at $x=0,$ since we cannot draw the points on the curve without taking our pen off the paper to cross the asymptote.\n\nSo, we say that $y = \\dfrac 1 x$ has a **discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote** at $x=0.$ Discontinuities due to vertical asymptotes are also called **infinite discontinuities**.\n\nNotice that here, both one-sided limits are infinite:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0^-} \\left(\\dfrac 1 x\\right) =-\\infty\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\\lim_{x\\to 0^+} \\left(\\dfrac 1 x \\right)=\\infty.\n$$\n\nIn general, a function $y=f(x)$ has a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=c$ if either one-sided limit is infinite:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to c^-} f(x) =\\pm \\infty\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad\\lim_{x\\to c^+} f(x) =\\pm \\infty.\n$$\n\nNote that we only need a single limit to become infinite to conclude that there is an infinite discontinuity at $x=c.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130330 #edb/ref 1065 2595 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130330 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ce83f36f-d7f8-546f-b887-1e6818a75bfb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130330 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504036 #edb/ref 1080 "x=3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504036 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504036 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "03c28aa6-a7c1-5d7d-9fc7-a54775494788" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504038 #edb/ref 1080 "$x=-2$ and $x=3$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504038 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504038 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "04967c23-79a8-538e-8874-cdcff4c1e152" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504040 #edb/ref 1080 "$x=-1$ and $x=2$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504040 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504040 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "054fd615-fb40-56d5-a49f-a713a52995ca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504054 #edb/ref 1080 "$x=1$ and $x=3$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504054 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504054 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0b46ada9-1c7a-5f43-8971-8d180ff44d81" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504061 #edb/ref 1080 "0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504061 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504061 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0ed3047e-cde1-5f5b-9213-d32a09a9276f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504066 #edb/ref 1080 "$x=3$ and $x=4$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504066 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504066 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "140e96dd-2459-59c5-bcaf-4fd8eead4254" #edb/ref 17592186045417]